    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">An entry level claims adjuster salary typically lands between $45,000 and $60,000 a year at an insurance carrier, with benefits, paid training and often a company vehicle for field roles. That is a solid start for a job that does not always require a degree, and it rarely stays there long. This guide covers what trainee pay looks like, what changes once you are licensed, and how earnings typically grow over your first five years.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Most entry-level and trainee claims adjusters earn about $45,000 to $60,000 a year. Pay usually rises after you complete training and get licensed, and by years three to five many adjusters earn $60,000 to $80,000 or more, especially in field property, casualty or workers comp roles.</div>

<h2 id="entry-level-claims-adjuster-salary-ranges">Entry level claims adjuster salary ranges</h2>
<p>Starting pay depends mostly on the line of business you begin in. Auto and desk roles tend to start lower; field property and casualty start a bit higher. The industry estimates below reflect what most trainee and first-year job postings we track offer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Auto / material damage trainee:</strong> commonly $45,000 to $55,000</li>
<li><strong>Desk property trainee:</strong> commonly $47,000 to $57,000</li>
<li><strong>Field property trainee:</strong> commonly $50,000 to $60,000, plus vehicle and gas card</li>
<li><strong>Casualty or workers comp trainee:</strong> commonly $48,000 to $60,000</li>
</ul>
<p>For context, the Bureau of Labor Statistics reports a median of $76,790 a year for claims adjusters, appraisers, examiners and investigators as of May 2024. New hires start below that median and typically reach it within a few years. See the full career range in our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/claims-adjuster-salary/">claims adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>

<h3>Desk vs field: where trainees start</h3>
<p>Desk trainees handle claims by phone, email and photos, often from a claim center or a remote setup. The work is high volume and teaches coverage and customer communication quickly. A property adjuster trainee assigned to the field spends more time driving to losses, climbing roofs and writing estimates on site, which is why field trainee pay and perks are usually a little higher. Neither path locks you in; many adjusters start on the desk and move to the field within two years.</p>

<h2 id="how-carrier-trainee-programs-work">How carrier trainee programs work</h2>
<p>Most large carriers run formal claims trainee programs. They are paid, full-time positions, and they are the most common way people break into the industry without prior experience. A typical program looks like this:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Classroom or virtual training (2 to 8 weeks).</strong> Policy language, coverage basics, claim systems and customer communication.</li>
<li><strong>Licensing.</strong> In states that require it, the carrier usually pays for your pre-licensing course and exam.</li>
<li><strong>Estimating training.</strong> Property and auto trainees learn Xactimate or the carrier's auto estimating platform.</li>
<li><strong>Supervised claim handling.</strong> You take a reduced caseload of simple claims with a mentor or team lead reviewing your files.</li>
<li><strong>Full caseload.</strong> Usually within 6 to 12 months, you handle a standard pending inventory on your own.</li>
</ol>
<p>The value is not just the paycheck. Trainees get benefits, a 401(k) match at many carriers, and a structured path that independent adjusters have to build on their own.</p>

<h2 id="what-first-year-pay-really-looks-like">What first-year adjuster pay really looks like</h2>
<p>Base salary is only part of a first-year package. Here is what a new adjuster commonly receives:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Base salary</strong> in the $45,000 to $60,000 range.</li>
<li><strong>Overtime or CAT pay.</strong> Many non-exempt trainee roles pay overtime, and storm duty can add meaningful income in a busy year.</li>
<li><strong>Company car, gas card, laptop and phone</strong> for field roles, which commonly adds $8,000 to $15,000 of value.</li>
<li><strong>Health, dental and retirement benefits.</strong></li>
<li><strong>Paid licensing and education costs.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>Compare a few offers side by side with the <a href="/salary-calculator/">Adjuster Salary Calculator</a>, which accounts for the value of a vehicle and benefits.</p>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask whether a trainee role is exempt or non-exempt. An hourly, overtime-eligible role at $50,000 base can out-earn a $55,000 salaried role during storm season.</div>

<h2 id="raises-after-licensing-and-designations">Raises after licensing and designations</h2>
<p>The first real bump usually comes when you finish training and hold your license. Many carriers tie a step increase to licensing, and some require it before you take a full caseload. After that, professional designations become the main lever:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>AIC (Associate in Claims).</strong> The most common claims designation. Many carriers reimburse the coursework and some award a raise or bonus on completion.</li>
<li><strong>CPCU (Chartered Property Casualty Underwriter).</strong> A more advanced, broader credential that signals readiness for senior, complex and leadership roles.</li>
<li><strong>Xactimate certification.</strong> Not a designation, but a practical skill credential that property employers and IA firms value highly.</li>
</ul>
<p>Licensing also opens doors outside your first employer. A license in a large reciprocity state, such as the Texas all-lines license that many adjusters use as a designated home state license, lets you add other states and makes you deployable for storm work later in your career.</p>
<p>Designations rarely double your pay overnight, but they make promotions and specialty transfers much easier to win.</p>

<h2 id="year-1-to-5-salary-progression">Year 1 to 5 salary progression</h2>
<p>Here is a typical progression for a staff adjuster who starts as a trainee and moves steadily toward field or specialty work. These are industry estimates; your employer, region and line of business will shift them.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Career stage</th><th>Typical role</th><th>Typical salary range</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Year 1</td><td>Trainee / entry-level adjuster</td><td>$45,000 - $60,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Year 2</td><td>Licensed desk or auto adjuster</td><td>$50,000 - $65,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Desk property, field auto or workers comp</td><td>$55,000 - $72,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Year 4</td><td>Field property or casualty adjuster</td><td>$62,000 - $82,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Year 5</td><td>Senior adjuster, AIC holder, specialty line</td><td>$68,000 - $90,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Beyond</td><td>Large loss, complex claims, supervisor</td><td>$85,000 - $120,000+</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<p>Some adjusters take a different route after two or three years: going independent. Experienced independent adjusters can earn $70,000 to $120,000 in an average active year, with much bigger swings in storm years, but they give up benefits and pay their own expenses. Our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/independent-adjuster-salary/">independent adjuster salary</a> guide explains the trade-off.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-get-hired-as-an-entry-level-adjuster">How to get hired as an entry-level adjuster</h2>
<p>Carriers hire trainees year-round, and there are thousands of openings each year from retirements and turnover, even as simple claims become more automated. To stand out:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Get licensed before you apply.</strong> A license shows commitment and shortens your training. Many candidates use an online pre-licensing course like <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a> (code joe20 takes 20% off).</li>
<li><strong>Lead with transferable experience.</strong> Customer service, roofing, construction, auto body, collections and claims support roles all translate well.</li>
<li><strong>Show you can write and organize.</strong> Adjusters live in documentation. A clean resume and clear cover letter matter.</li>
<li><strong>Be flexible on line and location.</strong> Auto and desk property roles hire the most trainees and are easier to land first.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare for scenario questions.</strong> Expect to explain how you would handle an upset customer or a claim with unclear facts.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Red flags in entry-level offers</h3>
<ul>
<li>Commission-only or per-claim pay with no training for someone with no experience.</li>
<li>Upfront fees to join a roster or receive a job, beyond your own licensing costs.</li>
<li>Vague answers about caseload size, supervision or how quickly you will be licensed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Legitimate carrier trainee jobs pay a salary from day one and invest in your training.</p>

<h2 id="is-an-entry-level-claims-adjuster-salary-worth-it">Is an entry level claims adjuster salary worth it?</h2>
<p>For most people, yes. You start with a paid, benefits-backed job, no student debt required, and a clear path into the $70,000 to $90,000 range within about five years if you specialize. The work is demanding, with heavy caseloads and frustrated customers, but the skills are portable across carriers, states and lines of business.</p>
<p>To see how your expected pay compares by location, check <a href="/salary-by-state/">insurance adjuster salary by state</a>, or start with our main <a href="/">insurance adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>
